## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of Android dynamic feature modules, remote config gating, and safe in-app upgrades with rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Feature Modules and SplitInstallManager
- Remote Config feature flags and gating
- Module metadata and compatibility checks
- Data migrations scoped to features
- Rollback strategies and robust test plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module metadata and compatibility check (illustrative)
class ModuleMeta { constructor(name, version, minAppVersion, minApi) {
  this.name = name; this.version = version; this.minAppVersion = minAppVersion; this.minApi = minApi;
}}
function isCompatible(meta, appVersion, deviceApi) {
  return appVersion >= meta.minAppVersion && deviceApi >= meta.minApi;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test upgrades across multiple device configurations and network conditions?
- How would you handle partial upgrades when device storage or network is constrained?

## Why This Is Asked

New angle introduces multi-cache offline view and scheduled refresh, probing data modeling, cache invalidation, and basic MVVM patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Room entities and relations
- Separate body cache design
- MVVM with Repository
- WorkManager with constraints
- Simple search SQL LIKE

## Code Example
```kotlin
@Entity(tableName = "article_meta")
data class ArticleMeta(
  @PrimaryKey val id: String,
  val title: String,
  val excerpt: String,
  val updatedAt: Long
)

@Entity(tableName = "article_body")
data class ArticleBody(
  @PrimaryKey val articleId: String,
  val body: String
)

@Dao
interface ArticleDao {
  @Query("SELECT * FROM article_meta WHERE title LIKE :query || '%'")
  fun searchByTitle(query: String): List<ArticleMeta>

  @Transaction
  @Query("SELECT * FROM article_meta WHERE id = :id")
  fun getArticleWithBody(id: String): ArticleWithBody
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle cache invalidation and staleness if the remote API updates articles?
- What tests would you write to verify offline viewing, search correctness, and refresh reliability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end encrypted, multi-party voice with dynamic membership, offline resilience, and production-ready media handling. It probes WebRTC, key management, and data persistence trade-offs across devices.

## Key Concepts

- WebRTC for multi-party audio and signaling flow
- End-to-end encryption with per-device keys and a group-key envelope
- Nightly key rotation via Android Keystore and secure key derivation
- Offline transcripts using Room; offline-first UX
- Audio quality: jitter buffer, AGC, and network adaptivity

